Array - Full Details (English + Hinglish)
What is an Array? (English)
An Array is a collection of elements stored in contiguous memory locations. It allows storing multiple values of the same data type under a single variable name. Arrays in Java are fixed in size, meaning once declared, their size cannot be changed.

Array Kya Hai? (Hinglish)
Array ek collection of elements hai jo continuous memory locations me store hote hain. Yeh ek same data type ke multiple values ko store kar sakta hai ek hi variable ke andar. Java me arrays ka size fixed hota hai, yaani ek baar declare hone ke baad iska size change nahi ho sakta.

Types of Arrays in Java
One-Dimensional Array (1D Array)
Multi-Dimensional Array (2D or 3D Array, etc.)
Jagged Array (Different length rows in a 2D array)

How to Declare an Array?
java
// Declaration (1D Array)
int[] arr;  
// OR
int arr[];

How to Initialize an Array?
1. Using Static Initialization

int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

2. Using Dynamic Initialization

int[] arr = new int[5];  // Creates an array of size 5
arr[0] = 10;  // Assigning values
arr[1] = 20;
📌 Note: Array indexing starts from 0 in Java.

Accessing Elements in an Array

System.out.println(arr[0]);  // Output: 10
System.out.println(arr[1]);  // Output: 20
🚨 If you access an index out of range, Java throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

Iterating Through an Array
1. Using a For Loop
for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
2. Using Enhanced For Loop
for(int num : arr) {
    System.out.println(num);
}
✅ Enhanced for loop is better for readability!

Multi-Dimensional Arrays (2D Array)
int[][] matrix = {
    {1, 2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6},
    {7, 8, 9}
};
Accessing a 2D Array Element
System.out.println(matrix[1][2]);  // Output: 6

Iterating Through a 2D Array
for(int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
    for(int j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {
        System.out.print(matrix[i][j] + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();
}
Jagged Arrays (Different Length Rows)
int[][] jaggedArr = new int[3][];
jaggedArr[0] = new int[2];  // Row 0 has 2 columns
jaggedArr[1] = new int[3];  // Row 1 has 3 columns
jaggedArr[2] = new int[1];  // Row 2 has 1 column

Array Methods in Java

Method/Property	Description
arr.length	Returns array size
Arrays.sort(arr)	Sorts an array
Arrays.fill(arr, val)	Fills array with a value
Arrays.toString(arr)	Converts array to string
Arrays.binarySearch(arr, key)	Searches for an element (sorted array)

Sorting an Array

import java.util.Arrays;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5, 2, 8, 1, 3};
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));  // Output: [1, 2, 3, 5, 8]
    }
}

Searching in an Array
1. Linear Search (O(n))

public static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int key) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == key) return i;
    }
    return -1;
}

2. Binary Search (O(log n), Sorted Array Required)

import java.util.Arrays;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 5, 8};
        int index = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 3);
        System.out.println(index);  // Output: 2
    }
}

Array vs ArrayList

Feature	Array	ArrayList
Size	Fixed	Dynamic
Performance	Faster	Slightly Slower
Primitive Types	Yes (int[], char[])	No (Only objects)
Memory Usage	Less	More
🔹 Use ArrayList when you need dynamic resizing.

Common Array Interview Questions

1.What is the difference between an array and a linked list?
Arrays have fixed size, linked lists have dynamic size.
Arrays allow random access, linked lists allow sequential access.

2.How to reverse an array?
public static void reverseArray(int[] arr) {
    int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        int temp = arr[left];
        arr[left] = arr[right];
        arr[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
}
3.How to find the maximum and minimum in an array?
public static void findMinMax(int[] arr) {
    int min = arr[0], max = arr[0];
    for (int num : arr) {
        if (num < min) min = num;
        if (num > max) max = num;
    }
    System.out.println("Min: " + min + ", Max: " + max);
}
